.TH CHTTPSVR_REQ_METHOD 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_req_method \- return the HTTP method of a request
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpserver.h>

chttp_method_t chttpsvr_req_method(const chttpsvr_req *req);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns the HTTP method of req as a chttp_method_t enum value.  The
return value is always one of the seven concrete methods (CHTTP_GET,
CHTTP_POST, CHTTP_PUT, CHTTP_DELETE, CHTTP_PATCH, CHTTP_HEAD,
CHTTP_OPTIONS); CHTTP_ANY is never returned, even when the matched
route was registered with CHTTP_ANY.

A request whose method the server does not recognize at all (a WebDAV
verb such as PROPFIND, TRACE, CONNECT, a custom verb, ...) never
reaches any handler in the first place, including one registered with
CHTTP_ANY: it is rejected with 501 Not Implemented before routing ever
runs (see chttpsvr_register_handler(3)'s own notes on CHTTP_ANY).  This
is what makes "always one of the seven concrete methods" an exhaustive
guarantee rather than merely the common case.

When req is NULL the function returns CHTTP_GET.  Unlike most other
request accessors (which return NULL for a NULL req), chttp_method_t is
an integer enum with no sentinel value, so callers must guard against
NULL before calling this function if req may be NULL.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_req_path (3),
.BR chttpsvr_req_header (3)
